<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Murong Chui</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">326–396</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Emperor Wucheng of Later Yan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-xianbei">Murong</a> Chui was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> prince of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/former-yan">Former Yan</a> who won fame as a general but fled to the Former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> in 369 to escape court intrigue. After Fu Jian's defeat at the Fei River in 383 he rebelled, proclaimed himself King of Yan in 384 and emperor in 386, founding the Later Yan with its capital at Zhongshan. He destroyed the Western Yan in 394 and died in 396.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/murong-chui.jpg" alt="Murong Chui" width="320" height="474" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: see below · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Harvard_drs_49802792_%E7%B9%A1%E5%83%8F%E4%B8%89%E5%9C%8B%E6%BC%94%E7%BE%A9%E7%BA%8C%E7%B7%A8_v.1_(%E6%85%95%E5%AE%B9%E5%9E%82).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Murong Chui, courtesy name Daoming, was born in 326, the fifth son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-huang">Murong Huang</a> by his concubine Consort Lan. His father favoured him and wished to make him heir, but accepted his officials' advice to choose the eldest son of his wife, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-jun">Murong Jun</a>, and instead named the boy Murong Ba, meaning hegemon. Murong Jun, jealous, renamed him after he succeeded in 348, first Murong Que and then Murong Chui. As a young general Murong Chui fought in the conquest of the Yuwen tribe in 344 and held the border against the Later Zhao.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-jun">Murong Jun</a> he helped take the eastern half of the former Later Zhao lands and was made Prince of Wu in 354, but the emperor kept him at a distance. In 358 his wife Princess Duan was falsely accused of witchcraft, tortured and died in prison. Murong Chui helped the regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-ke">Murong Ke</a> capture <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> in 365, and in 369 he and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-de">Murong De</a> defeated the Jin general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huan-wen">Huan Wen</a> near Yecheng. The regent Murong Ping and Empress Dowager Kezuhun then considered executing him, and he fled.</p><p>Fu Jian of the Former Qin welcomed him and made him Marquess of Bintu, despite the doubts of the prime minister Wang Meng. In 370 Wang Meng tricked Murong Chui's son Murong Ling into defecting back to Yan, but Fu Jian pardoned the father. When Fu Jian's army collapsed at the Fei River in 383, Murong Chui's troops stayed intact and Fu Jian took refuge with him; Murong Chui refused his son's and brother's urging to kill him, before turning to rebellion himself.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After taking Yecheng late in 385 he made Zhongshan his capital. In 386 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-daowu-of-northern-wei">Tuoba Gui</a> of the Northern Wei became his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, but relations broke down in 391. Murong Chui conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingling">Dingling</a> state of Wei at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hua-county">Huatai</a> in 392 and then took Zhangzi, capital of the Western Yan, killing its emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-yong">Murong Yong</a>.</p><p>After Northern Wei destroyed a Later Yan army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-canhe-slope">Battle of Canhe Slope</a> in 395, he led a new campaign and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/datong">Pingcheng</a>, but on passing the battlefield, with its bodies, he fell gravely ill and died at Shanggu on 2 June 396. Historians count him a general who never lost a battle, but blame him for betraying Fu Jian and for leaving a weak state to his heir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-bao">Murong Bao</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Murong Chui” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 395 the Yan emperor <b>Murong Chui</b> sent his crown prince Murong Bao with a massive punitive army, and Tuoba Gui crushed it at the Battle of Canhe Slope.</blockquote><a href="/empire/northern-wei">Northern Wei · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After Murong Ke died, court jealousy drove out the talented general <b>Murong Chui</b>, who had defeated Huan Wen at Fangtou in 369; he fled to Former Qin.</blockquote><a href="/state/former-yan">Former Yan</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Murong Chui</b>, a Former Yan prince who had served Former Qin, rebelled after the Fei River defeat, proclaimed himself King of Yan in 384 and emperor in 386, with his capital at Zhongshan (modern Dingzhou, Hebei).</blockquote><a href="/state/later-yan">Later Yan</a></li><li><blockquote>When Northern Wei took the Later Yan capital Zhongshan in 397, Murong De, a younger brother of <b>Murong Chui</b>, led followers south to Huatai, proclaiming himself King of Yan in 398.</blockquote><a href="/state/southern-yan">Southern Yan</a></li><li><blockquote>Murong Yong refused to submit to his relative <b>Murong Chui of Later Yan</b>, who regarded himself as the rightful heir of the Yan line.</blockquote><a href="/state/western-yan">Western Yan</a></li><li><blockquote>In 392 <b>Murong Chui of Later Yan</b> forced a crossing of the Yellow River, defeated Zhai Zhao and took Huatai.</blockquote><a href="/state/zhai-wei">Zhai Wei</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Yi County, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.53,121.24&amp;year=371" aria-label="Yi County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Murong Chui"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5079 4020h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5079 4020h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.53,121.24&amp;year=371">What was here in 371 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
